  "notes": "Describes the state of ground that has absorbed water and become soft, squishy, and difficult to traverse. Almost always used about outdoor surfaces like roads, paths, fields, and gardens. Most commonly encountered in the ている form (ぬかるんでいる) to describe the resulting muddy state.\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {道|みち}がぬかるむ: the road becomes muddy\n- {地面|じめん}がぬかるむ: the ground becomes muddy\n- {足元|あしもと}がぬかるむ: the footing becomes muddy\n- ぬかるんだ{道|みち}: a muddy road\n\nRELATED WORDS:\n- ぬかるみ: mud; muddy place (the noun form)\n- {泥|どろ}: mud\n- {泥濘|ぬかるみ}: muddy ground (literary/formal spelling of ぬかるみ)",
